description:
Sha256::from_bytes(&[u8; 32]) -> Self (crate-jmap-cid-types/src/digest.rs:133) is infallible — every byte produces two valid lowercase-hex nibbles by construction. Idiomatic Rust says: infallible conversion = From impl; named constructor only when the conversion is ambiguous or fallible.

Distinct from myoss finding .4: .4 flags the *name* (from_bytes reads as 'compute digest of bytes'). This flags the *trait shape*. Both can be fixed independently — even if from_bytes is renamed, it should still be expressed as a trait impl. The two together suggest the final shape:

    impl From<&[u8; 32]> for Sha256 { ... }
    // and optionally
    impl From<[u8; 32]> for Sha256 { ... }

Then callers write Sha256::from(digest_bytes) or digest_bytes.into() — both self-documenting at the call site, both unambiguous about the direction of conversion. The Sha256::from_bytes(&bytes) call site reads identically to the proposed Sha256::from(&bytes), so the migration is essentially a name change plus a trait impl.

What goes wrong by leaving as-is:
- Callers cannot use  at the call site for a conversion that is *literally* what From exists for.
- The Sha256 surface advertises a named constructor where a trait impl would document intent through the type system.
- Diverges from the wider Rust ecosystem convention for digest types (RustCrypto's Output<T> uses From<GenericArray<u8, N>>).
